{"id": "summarization-001", "clusterId": "summarization", "prompt": [{"role": "user", "content": "Summarize this internal email in 3 bullet points for a busy executive.\n\n--- TEXT ---\nFrom: Lena Marsh, Engineering Manager\nTo: mobile-team\nDate: May 3\n\nTeam — quick update on the Halcyon app release. QA finished regression testing on Thursday and found two blocker bugs: one in offline sync and one in the login flow. Both are now fixed and verified. Because of the fixes, we are pushing the release from May 12 to May 19. The App Store listing copy is final; screenshots still need retaking on the new build, and Priya owns that by Wednesday. Beta feedback on the new dashboard has been 92% positive, so we're keeping it as the default home screen. No all-hands next week; we'll review the launch checklist in the usual Monday standup instead."}], "scoring": {"kind": "llm-judge", "rubric": "Score the summary against these criteria: (1) Faithfulness — every claim appears in the source, no hallucinated facts or numbers; (2) Coverage — includes the key facts: two blocker bugs (offline sync, login) fixed; release moved from May 12 to May 19; Priya retakes screenshots by Wednesday; dashboard stays default (92% positive beta feedback); no all-hands next week; (3) Concision — follows the requested length/format without padding; (4) Readability — clear, grammatical, stands alone. 10 = all four fully met. 7-9 = minor omission or slight wordiness. 4-6 = misses a key fact or adds one unsupported detail. 1-3 = misses most key facts or contains multiple unsupported claims. 0 = unrelated or mostly fabricated.", "judgeModel": "judge-class", "scale": [0, 10]}}
{"id": "summarization-002", "clusterId": "summarization", "prompt": [{"role": "user", "content": "Write a 2-3 sentence summary of this incident report suitable for a status page.\n\n--- TEXT ---\nINCIDENT REPORT — IR-2026-041\nService: Atlas Search API. Window: 02:14-03:47 UTC, August 12.\nSummary: Elevated error rates (peaking at 38% of requests) affected the Atlas Search API for 93 minutes. The root cause was a configuration change that set the cache TTL to 0 seconds, causing every query to hit the primary database and exhaust its connection pool. The on-call engineer rolled back the change at 03:41; error rates returned to baseline within 6 minutes. Approximately 41,000 requests failed. No data was lost. Action items: (1) add a config-validation gate to the deploy pipeline, (2) raise the connection-pool alarm threshold sensitivity, (3) schedule a blameless retro for August 18."}], "scoring": {"kind": "llm-judge", "rubric": "Score the summary against these criteria: (1) Faithfulness — every claim appears in the source, no hallucinated facts or numbers; (2) Coverage — includes the key facts: 93-minute outage of Atlas Search API on Aug 12; cause was cache TTL set to 0, exhausting the DB connection pool; fixed by rollback; no data lost; (3) Concision — follows the requested length/format without padding; (4) Readability — clear, grammatical, stands alone. 10 = all four fully met. 7-9 = minor omission or slight wordiness. 4-6 = misses a key fact or adds one unsupported detail. 1-3 = misses most key facts or contains multiple unsupported claims. 0 = unrelated or mostly fabricated.", "judgeModel": "judge-class", "scale": [0, 10]}}
{"id": "summarization-003", "clusterId": "summarization", "prompt": [{"role": "user", "content": "Summarize this research abstract in one sentence (max 40 words) for a newsletter.\n\n--- TEXT ---\nABSTRACT — Journal of Coastal Ecology (fictional)\nWe monitored nesting success of the dune lark (Calendulauda dunensis) across 14 sites on the Alderney coast over three breeding seasons. Sites with restored marram grass cover showed a mean nesting success of 61%, compared with 34% at unrestored sites. Predation by gulls accounted for 58% of nest failures overall, but only 29% at restored sites, suggesting vegetation cover primarily reduces predation rather than improving food availability. Trail camera data indicated human disturbance within 50 m of nests reduced fledging rates by roughly a third regardless of site restoration. We recommend pairing vegetation restoration with seasonal 50 m buffer zones around active colonies."}], "scoring": {"kind": "llm-judge", "rubric": "Score the summary against these criteria: (1) Faithfulness — every claim appears in the source, no hallucinated facts or numbers; (2) Coverage — includes the key facts: restored grass cover raised dune lark nesting success (61% vs 34%) mainly by reducing gull predation; human disturbance within 50 m cut fledging by a third; authors recommend restoration plus 50 m buffer zones; (3) Concision — follows the requested length/format without padding; (4) Readability — clear, grammatical, stands alone. 10 = all four fully met. 7-9 = minor omission or slight wordiness. 4-6 = misses a key fact or adds one unsupported detail. 1-3 = misses most key facts or contains multiple unsupported claims. 0 = unrelated or mostly fabricated.", "judgeModel": "judge-class", "scale": [0, 10]}}
{"id": "summarization-004", "clusterId": "summarization", "prompt": [{"role": "user", "content": "Summarize this support thread in exactly 2 sentences.\n\n--- TEXT ---\nSUPPORT THREAD — Ticket #4471\nCustomer (Jun 2): My Fernway kettle (order 11829) stopped heating after two weeks. Light comes on, water stays cold.\nAgent (Jun 2): Sorry to hear that. Could you confirm the outlet works with another appliance, and whether the base shows any visible damage?\nCustomer (Jun 3): Outlet is fine, no visible damage. I also tried a different outlet — same result.\nAgent (Jun 3): Thanks for checking. This sounds like a failed heating element, which is covered under the 2-year warranty. I've approved a replacement unit; it ships today and should arrive June 7. No need to return the defective unit; please recycle it locally.\nCustomer (Jun 4): Replacement approved already? Great, thanks. Tracking number when you have it?\nAgent (Jun 4): Tracking: FW-88213-Q. Anything else I can help with?"}], "scoring": {"kind": "llm-judge", "rubric": "Score the summary against these criteria: (1) Faithfulness — every claim appears in the source, no hallucinated facts or numbers; (2) Coverage — includes the key facts: kettle failed to heat after two weeks; troubleshooting ruled out outlet/damage; agent approved a warranty replacement arriving June 7 with no return required; tracking FW-88213-Q provided; (3) Concision — follows the requested length/format without padding; (4) Readability — clear, grammatical, stands alone. 10 = all four fully met. 7-9 = minor omission or slight wordiness. 4-6 = misses a key fact or adds one unsupported detail. 1-3 = misses most key facts or contains multiple unsupported claims. 0 = unrelated or mostly fabricated.", "judgeModel": "judge-class", "scale": [0, 10]}}
{"id": "summarization-005", "clusterId": "summarization", "prompt": [{"role": "user", "content": "Summarize these release notes in a short bulleted list, grouping breaking changes, new features, and fixes.\n\n--- TEXT ---\nRELEASE NOTES — Quillmark 2.4.0\nBreaking: The legacy RTF parser has been removed. Migrate to parseRichText() before upgrading; the old parseRTF() entry point now throws. New: Table column alignment in PDF export now respects the alignment property. New: Export jobs can be cancelled mid-run via jobs.cancel(id). Fixed: Footnote numbering no longer restarts after page 256. Fixed: Memory leak when exporting documents over 400 pages (heap usage down ~60% in our benchmarks). Deprecated: setTheme('classic') is deprecated and will be removed in 3.0; use setTheme('legacy') for the same appearance."}], "scoring": {"kind": "llm-judge", "rubric": "Score the summary against these criteria: (1) Faithfulness — every claim appears in the source, no hallucinated facts or numbers; (2) Coverage — includes the key facts: breaking: legacy RTF parser removed, migrate to parseRichText; new: PDF table alignment, cancellable export jobs; fixes: footnote numbering past page 256, large-document memory leak; deprecation: setTheme('classic'); (3) Concision — follows the requested length/format without padding; (4) Readability — clear, grammatical, stands alone. 10 = all four fully met. 7-9 = minor omission or slight wordiness. 4-6 = misses a key fact or adds one unsupported detail. 1-3 = misses most key facts or contains multiple unsupported claims. 0 = unrelated or mostly fabricated.", "judgeModel": "judge-class", "scale": [0, 10]}}
{"id": "summarization-006", "clusterId": "summarization", "prompt": [{"role": "user", "content": "Summarize this excerpt in 2 sentences for a local news brief.\n\n--- TEXT ---\nTOWN COUNCIL MINUTES (excerpt) — Bellwether, Sept 9\nItem 7: Riverside path lighting. Councilor Ito presented the petition (1,240 signatures) requesting lights on the unlit 800 m stretch of the riverside path. The parks department estimated installation at $86,000 and annual upkeep at $7,500. Councilor Brandt proposed solar studs instead, at roughly half the install cost. After discussion, the council voted 5-2 to fund solar stud installation in the next fiscal year, with a review of effectiveness after 12 months. Ito and Marsh voted against, preferring full lighting."}], "scoring": {"kind": "llm-judge", "rubric": "Score the summary against these criteria: (1) Faithfulness — every claim appears in the source, no hallucinated facts or numbers; (2) Coverage — includes the key facts: council voted 5-2 to install solar studs (about half the $86,000 cost of full lighting) on an unlit 800 m riverside path stretch after a 1,240-signature petition; effectiveness review after 12 months; Ito and Marsh opposed; (3) Concision — follows the requested length/format without padding; (4) Readability — clear, grammatical, stands alone. 10 = all four fully met. 7-9 = minor omission or slight wordiness. 4-6 = misses a key fact or adds one unsupported detail. 1-3 = misses most key facts or contains multiple unsupported claims. 0 = unrelated or mostly fabricated.", "judgeModel": "judge-class", "scale": [0, 10]}}
{"id": "summarization-007", "clusterId": "summarization", "prompt": [{"role": "user", "content": "Summarize this letter in 3 bullets, keeping all headline metrics.\n\n--- TEXT ---\nQUARTERLY LETTER TO SELLERS — Northwind Marketplace, Q3\nGross merchandise volume grew 14% quarter-over-quarter to $212M, our strongest quarter since launch. Active sellers reached 31,400, up 9%. The new bulk-listing tool, launched mid-quarter, already accounts for 22% of new listings. Returns ticked up from 3.1% to 3.8% of orders, driven mostly by apparel; we are piloting clearer size charts with 200 apparel sellers in Q4. Seller payout speed improved: 96% of payouts now land within 2 business days, versus 88% last quarter. In Q4 we will focus on the size-chart pilot, a revamped seller dashboard, and expanding next-day delivery coverage to 12 more metro areas."}], "scoring": {"kind": "llm-judge", "rubric": "Score the summary against these criteria: (1) Faithfulness — every claim appears in the source, no hallucinated facts or numbers; (2) Coverage — includes the key facts: GMV +14% to $212M; 31,400 active sellers (+9%); bulk-listing tool = 22% of new listings; returns up to 3.8% (apparel-driven, size-chart pilot in Q4); 96% of payouts within 2 business days (was 88%); (3) Concision — follows the requested length/format without padding; (4) Readability — clear, grammatical, stands alone. 10 = all four fully met. 7-9 = minor omission or slight wordiness. 4-6 = misses a key fact or adds one unsupported detail. 1-3 = misses most key facts or contains multiple unsupported claims. 0 = unrelated or mostly fabricated.", "judgeModel": "judge-class", "scale": [0, 10]}}
{"id": "summarization-008", "clusterId": "summarization", "prompt": [{"role": "user", "content": "Summarize this excerpt as a checklist of at most 5 items for a new hire.\n\n--- TEXT ---\nONBOARDING DOC (excerpt) — Corvid Robotics, IT setup\nNew hires receive their laptop on day one from the IT desk (Building C, 2nd floor, 9:00-11:00). Sign in with the temporary password from your welcome letter; you'll be forced to change it at first login. VPN access requires enrolling a phone in Duo via the link emailed by IT — this takes about 10 minutes and must be done before your first remote day. The engineering wiki and ticket tracker use single sign-on; access is provisioned automatically within 24 hours of your start date. If anything isn't working after 24 hours, file a ticket with IT-HELP rather than emailing individuals."}], "scoring": {"kind": "llm-judge", "rubric": "Score the summary against these criteria: (1) Faithfulness — every claim appears in the source, no hallucinated facts or numbers; (2) Coverage — includes the key facts: pick up laptop day one at IT desk (Bldg C, 2nd floor, 9-11); change temporary password at first login; enroll phone in Duo before first remote day; SSO access to wiki/tracker auto-provisioned within 24h; file IT-HELP ticket if issues persist after 24h; (3) Concision — follows the requested length/format without padding; (4) Readability — clear, grammatical, stands alone. 10 = all four fully met. 7-9 = minor omission or slight wordiness. 4-6 = misses a key fact or adds one unsupported detail. 1-3 = misses most key facts or contains multiple unsupported claims. 0 = unrelated or mostly fabricated.", "judgeModel": "judge-class", "scale": [0, 10]}}
{"id": "summarization-009", "clusterId": "summarization", "prompt": [{"role": "user", "content": "Summarize this update in 2-3 sentences for the app-store 'What's New' field.\n\n--- TEXT ---\nPRODUCT UPDATE — Fernhill Bank mobile app, v5.8\nThis release adds spending insights: a monthly breakdown of card spending by category, with comparisons to your three-month average. Insights appear on the home screen for all users; no opt-in needed. We also redesigned the transfers flow — it now takes three taps instead of six, and saved payees sync across devices immediately (previously up to an hour). Behind the scenes we dropped support for devices running OS versions older than 14; roughly 1.2% of active devices will no longer receive updates. Biometric login reliability improved in our testing, cutting failed attempts by about 40%."}], "scoring": {"kind": "llm-judge", "rubric": "Score the summary against these criteria: (1) Faithfulness — every claim appears in the source, no hallucinated facts or numbers; (2) Coverage — includes the key facts: new spending insights on home screen; transfers redesigned (3 taps vs 6, instant payee sync); biometric login failures down ~40%; drops support for OS older than 14; (3) Concision — follows the requested length/format without padding; (4) Readability — clear, grammatical, stands alone. 10 = all four fully met. 7-9 = minor omission or slight wordiness. 4-6 = misses a key fact or adds one unsupported detail. 1-3 = misses most key facts or contains multiple unsupported claims. 0 = unrelated or mostly fabricated.", "judgeModel": "judge-class", "scale": [0, 10]}}
{"id": "summarization-010", "clusterId": "summarization", "prompt": [{"role": "user", "content": "Summarize the triage outcome in 2 sentences.\n\n--- TEXT ---\nBUG REPORT TRIAGE NOTES — Tracker issue CORV-884\nReporter: warehouse scanner app freezes when scanning a pallet with more than 200 items. Reproduced on build 3.11.2; not reproducible on 3.10.9. Triage discussion: Maya suspects the new item-grouping diff introduced in 3.11, which is O(n^2) in the worst case. Jonah notes the freeze occurs even with grouping disabled, so the regression range may be wider. Decision: assign to Maya, priority P1, target fix in 3.11.3 next week. Jonah will bisect builds between 3.10.9 and 3.11.2 to confirm the offending commit. QA to add a 500-item pallet regression test to the suite."}], "scoring": {"kind": "llm-judge", "rubric": "Score the summary against these criteria: (1) Faithfulness — every claim appears in the source, no hallucinated facts or numbers; (2) Coverage — includes the key facts: scanner app freezes on 200+ item pallets (regression between 3.10.9 and 3.11.2, cause unconfirmed); assigned to Maya as P1 for 3.11.3 with Jonah bisecting builds and QA adding a 500-item regression test; (3) Concision — follows the requested length/format without padding; (4) Readability — clear, grammatical, stands alone. 10 = all four fully met. 7-9 = minor omission or slight wordiness. 4-6 = misses a key fact or adds one unsupported detail. 1-3 = misses most key facts or contains multiple unsupported claims. 0 = unrelated or mostly fabricated.", "judgeModel": "judge-class", "scale": [0, 10]}}
{"id": "summarization-011", "clusterId": "summarization", "prompt": [{"role": "user", "content": "Summarize the speaker's practical advice in 3 bullets.\n\n--- TEXT ---\nINTERVIEW TRANSCRIPT (excerpt) — urban gardener Reza Adeyemi on balcony composting\n\"I started with a 20-liter worm bin under the sink. People think it smells — it doesn't, if you bury the scraps and keep the moisture right, like a wrung-out sponge. The mistake everyone makes is overfeeding; worms need a week to work through a kilogram of scraps, and beginners dump three kilos in at once. My balcony setup now handles about 80% of our kitchen waste, maybe four kilos a week, and I harvest castings every two months for the tomatoes. City rules here allow bins up to 40 liters on balconies, which surprises people. Start small, weigh your scraps for a week, and size the bin to that."}], "scoring": {"kind": "llm-judge", "rubric": "Score the summary against these criteria: (1) Faithfulness — every claim appears in the source, no hallucinated facts or numbers; (2) Coverage — includes the key facts: bury scraps and keep moisture like a wrung-out sponge to avoid odor; don't overfeed (worms process ~1 kg/week); weigh scraps for a week and size the bin accordingly (balcony bins up to 40 L allowed locally); (3) Concision — follows the requested length/format without padding; (4) Readability — clear, grammatical, stands alone. 10 = all four fully met. 7-9 = minor omission or slight wordiness. 4-6 = misses a key fact or adds one unsupported detail. 1-3 = misses most key facts or contains multiple unsupported claims. 0 = unrelated or mostly fabricated.", "judgeModel": "judge-class", "scale": [0, 10]}}
{"id": "summarization-012", "clusterId": "summarization", "prompt": [{"role": "user", "content": "Summarize the policy changes in a short bulleted list for a library notice board.\n\n--- TEXT ---\nPOLICY MEMO — Aldergate University Library, effective October 1\nLoan periods change October 1: standard books move from 4 weeks to 6 weeks, with two renewals permitted (previously three). High-demand course reserves remain 3-hour loans. Overdue fines are eliminated for standard loans; instead, borrowing privileges pause after an item is 14 days overdue and resume on return. Fines remain for course reserves ($1/hour). Interlibrary loan requests now cap at 10 active requests per patron (previously unlimited). Laptops and chargers remain 4-hour, no renewal. The memo notes the changes follow a student senate survey in which 78% favored longer loans over renewals."}], "scoring": {"kind": "llm-judge", "rubric": "Score the summary against these criteria: (1) Faithfulness — every claim appears in the source, no hallucinated facts or numbers; (2) Coverage — includes the key facts: standard loans 4 to 6 weeks, renewals 3 to 2; overdue fines eliminated for standard loans (privileges pause after 14 days overdue); reserve fines unchanged ($1/hr); ILL cap of 10 active requests; laptops unchanged; (3) Concision — follows the requested length/format without padding; (4) Readability — clear, grammatical, stands alone. 10 = all four fully met. 7-9 = minor omission or slight wordiness. 4-6 = misses a key fact or adds one unsupported detail. 1-3 = misses most key facts or contains multiple unsupported claims. 0 = unrelated or mostly fabricated.", "judgeModel": "judge-class", "scale": [0, 10]}}
{"id": "summarization-013", "clusterId": "summarization", "prompt": [{"role": "user", "content": "Summarize the protocol's liveness and security rules in 2-3 sentences.\n\n--- TEXT ---\nSPEC EXCERPT — Draft v0.3, 'Beacon' presence protocol\nA Beacon node announces itself every 30 seconds over multicast group 239.44.10.9, port 8771. An announcement contains the node ID (16 bytes), a monotonically increasing epoch counter, and a capability bitmask. A node is considered live by peers if an announcement arrives within 90 seconds of the previous one (three missed intervals). On epoch rollover (2^31), nodes must rejoin with epoch 1 and a new random session salt. Announcements are not encrypted in v0.3; deployments on untrusted networks should use the optional HMAC trailer defined in section 6. Nodes with the relay bit set must forward announcements to directly connected subnets within 500 ms."}], "scoring": {"kind": "llm-judge", "rubric": "Score the summary against these criteria: (1) Faithfulness — every claim appears in the source, no hallucinated facts or numbers; (2) Coverage — includes the key facts: nodes announce every 30 s on multicast 239.44.10.9:8771; a peer is dead after three missed intervals (90 s); epoch rollover at 2^31 requires rejoining with a new salt; announcements are unencrypted by default, with an optional HMAC trailer for untrusted networks; (3) Concision — follows the requested length/format without padding; (4) Readability — clear, grammatical, stands alone. 10 = all four fully met. 7-9 = minor omission or slight wordiness. 4-6 = misses a key fact or adds one unsupported detail. 1-3 = misses most key facts or contains multiple unsupported claims. 0 = unrelated or mostly fabricated.", "judgeModel": "judge-class", "scale": [0, 10]}}
{"id": "summarization-014", "clusterId": "summarization", "prompt": [{"role": "user", "content": "Summarize this digest in one paragraph of at most 60 words for a team standup.\n\n--- TEXT ---\nCUSTOMER FEEDBACK DIGEST — Loomly Calendar, week of Aug 4\nWe tagged 312 feedback items this week. Top themes: (1) Time-zone handling for recurring events (74 items) — events shift an hour after DST changes; (2) Request for a shared family calendar view (58 items); (3) Slack integration requests (41 items), mostly wanting meeting reminders in channels; (4) Praise for the new keyboard shortcuts (37 items); (5) Complaints about mobile widget lag on older phones (22 items). Sentiment overall: 61% positive, 24% mixed, 15% negative — negative share down from 21% last week, largely because the sync bug from the July 28 release was patched on Aug 1."}], "scoring": {"kind": "llm-judge", "rubric": "Score the summary against these criteria: (1) Faithfulness — every claim appears in the source, no hallucinated facts or numbers; (2) Coverage — includes the key facts: 312 items; top request is fixing DST shifts on recurring events (74), then family calendar view (58) and Slack reminders (41); shortcuts praised; sentiment 61% positive with negative share down from 21% after the sync-bug patch; (3) Concision — follows the requested length/format without padding; (4) Readability — clear, grammatical, stands alone. 10 = all four fully met. 7-9 = minor omission or slight wordiness. 4-6 = misses a key fact or adds one unsupported detail. 1-3 = misses most key facts or contains multiple unsupported claims. 0 = unrelated or mostly fabricated.", "judgeModel": "judge-class", "scale": [0, 10]}}
